<<<
but. 1. Case after b. = except. 2. Redundant negative after 5. 8. Illogical b. 4. Wheels within wheels. 5. B.... however. 6. But which.
===
**but. 1. Case after *b.* = except. 2. Redundant negative after *b.* 3. Illogical *b.* 4. Wheels within wheels. 5. *B* . . . *however*. 6. *But which*.**
>>>

<<<
1. Case after but = except. The question is whether b. in this sense is a preposition,
===
**1. Case after *but* = except.** The question is whether *b.* in this sense is a preposition,
>>>

<<<
always take an objective case (Noone saw him but me, as well as I saw mo-one but him), or whether it is a conjunction,
===
always take an objective case (*No-one saw him but me*, as well as *I saw no-one but him*), or whether it is a conjunction,
>>>

<<<
the case after it is therefore variable (I saw no-one but him, i.e. but I did see him; No-one saw him but I, i.e. but I did see him).
===
the case after it is therefore variable (*I saw no-one but him*, i.e. but I did see *him*; *No-one saw him but I*, i.e. but *I* did see him).
>>>

<<<
The answer is that but was originally a preposition meaning outside, but is now usually made @ conjunction,
===
The answer is that *but* was originally a preposition meaning outside, but is now usually made a conjunction,
>>>

